            <div xml:id="d225">

                <pb facs="0395" n="271" xml:id="pg271"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.63119/3: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commissioner at Vienna</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Frazier</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-08-12T12:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 12,
                                1921—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received August 13?—1:11
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>148. Referring to my number 145 Aug. 10, 9 a.m.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d225-f1"/>
                    The following communication received from Federal Chancellor this morning:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The Federal Government is firmly resolved to consider the rights,
                            interests and advantages derived from the Treaty of Saint Germain and
                            especially from parts 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, and 14 of that Treaty,
                            also as strictly for the benefit of the United States. It is evident
                            that my foregoing assertion applies also to the participation of the
                            U[nited] S[tates] in the Reparations Commission and in any other
                            commission which may have been or may be established under the
                            provisions of the Treaty of Saint Germain so far as the United States
                            may desire to participate. The Federal Government, far from raising any
                            objection, would welcome a participation of the United States in such
                            commissions the more gladly as it confidently hopes that the United
                            States’s taking an active part in the work of these bodies could only be
                            for the sake of general peace and welfare.”</p></quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Frazier</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
